Transformer-based architectures have also been applied to molecular representation learning, similarly stemming from NLP ideas. MolBERT applies a bidirectional transformer to learn molecular representations from SMILES strings through self-supervised pre-training \citep{Fabian2020}. The model has domain-relevant auxiliary adaptations, such as the prediction of calculated physicochemical properties alongside the masked token prediction, to improve the chemical fidelity of the learned embeddings \citep{Fabian2020}. MolBERT outperformed ECFP4, RDKit descriptors and the CDDD neural representation on virtual screening and QSAR benchmarks \citep{Fabian2020}. ChemBERTa is another transformer-based approach which pre-trains masked language models on SMILES data from PubChem \citep{Chithrananda2020}.  ChemBERTa uses a byte-pair-encoding tokenizer by default and compares it with a SMILES-specific tokenizer \citep{Chithrananda2020}. Attention visualization shows neurons selective for chemically relevant functional groups and aromatic rings, and others that track bracket closures in the SMILES string \citep{Chithrananda2020}. ChemBERTa-2, pre-trained on up to 77 million PubChem compounds, is competitive with existing architectures on MoleculeNet \citep{Ahmad2022}.

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Jaeger2018 presents 'pretrained once' as an advantage, not cost; Chithrananda2020 does not call them black boxes.
Neural fingerprints provide several advantages over traditional fingerprints. They produce dense continuous vectors that avoid sparseness and information loss from bit collisions \citep{Jaeger2018}. They can also be pre-trained once on large unlabeled datasets and then fine-tuned for specific downstream tasks, opening the door to transfer learning \citep{Chithrananda2020, Fabian2020}. However, pre-training them is computationally expensive, and they are less directly interpretable than fingerprints, although attention visualization offers some insight \citep{Chithrananda2020}.

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Cherkasov2014 says it is difficult to discern a clear physical-chemical interpretation for many descriptors.
Beyond fingerprints, physicochemical-descriptor vectors (PDVs) are a class of continuous-value molecular descriptors that encode structural and chemical properties of molecules \citep{Todeschini2009}. Unlike binary fingerprints or graph-based representations, PDVs aggregate atom and bond-level quantities into fixed-length feature vectors that capture a wide variety of information about a molecule and its structure \citep{Mauri2006}. PDVs typically consist of hundreds of properties including constitutional descriptors such as molecular weight, atom and bond counts, and heteroatom ratios; topological indices encoding molecular connectivity patterns; physicochemical properties such as logP (lipophilicity), molar refractivity, and topological polar surface area; and electronic descriptors derived from partial charges and polarizabilities \citep{Todeschini2009, Cherkasov2014}. The \texttt{RDKit} cheminformatics toolkit provides a comprehensive suite of more than 200 molecular descriptors that cover these categories \citep{rdkit}. Many PDV descriptors, such as logP, molecular weight and partial charges, have a direct physicochemical meaning, although many of the thousands available have no clear interpretation \citep{Cherkasov2014}. They are also less computationally expensive than some 3D methods or neural fingerprints, with many descriptors simply requiring a 2D molecular structure without conformer generation or extensive pre-training. This efficiency makes PDVs particularly appropriate for high-throughput applications. Descriptors based on 3D structure can also be used \citep{Mauri2006}.

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Mauri2006 (DRAGON software) does not make this comparison; the sentence is our own reasoning.
However, PDVs have limitations. The dimensionality of PDV feature spaces can lead to problems with multicollinearity \citep{Todeschini2009}. This can be mitigated by feature selection or dimensionality reduction techniques \citep{Cherkasov2014}. Additionally, PDVs encode molecular properties as aggregated global features, which may not capture fine-grained structural details such as specific substructure arrangements or local chemical environments, information important for particular endpoints.

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: matched to Zhichun2023 Table 1; if the earlier lists were our own features, state them without this citation.
One of the more intuitive representations is the molecular graph. These graphs can be categorized into 2D and 3D graphs. 2D graphs rely on adjacency matrices that encode topological atom- and bond-level features but leave out geometric properties such as bond angles or torsion, meaning they may fail to capture stereochemistry. 3D graphs incorporate these spatial features, but are often limited by remaining in a single conformation \citep{Zhichun2023}. A standard molecular graph is constructed such that each molecule is represented as $G = (V,E)$ with node features $\mathbf{x}_i$ and edge features $\mathbf{e}_{ij}$. In one common featurization, nodes encode atom type, chirality, hybridization and aromaticity, and edges encode bond type, ring membership, direction and stereochemistry \citep{Zhichun2023}. 

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: 'standard distance compatibility molecular graph' is not in David2020; Hnig2022 reviews superposition methods (full text not checked).
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: voxel sparsity is a property of 3D grids, not graphs (Kuzminykh2018).
In the standard molecular graph, atoms are mapped to nodes and bonds to edges \citep{David2020}. These molecular graphs are typically 2D objects that represent 3D information such as atomic coordinates, bond angles, chirality, etc. \citep{David2020}. However, there are alternative molecular graph representations such as graphs with quantum 3D embeddings, weight alignment graphs, association graphs containing pairs of molecules, and geometric interaction graph neural networks that incorporate 3D structures and physical interactions \citep{Hnig2022, yan2023, Yang2023GIGN}. \citet{Du2022} developed hybrid 2D and 3D graphs, combining 3D data, guaranteeing precision and completeness by providing unabridged information, and 2D data, providing chemical intuitions for guidance, in a zipper-like arrangement. Voxel-based 3D representations, in which each atom occupies one grid cell, are too sparse for convolutional networks to perform well; \citet{Kuzminykh2018} instead spread each atom over nearby voxels with a wave transform.
      
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: ross2022 (MoLFormer) is a SMILES transformer, not a GNN.
These graph representations do not highlight specific substructures or specific arrangements of atoms and bonds within each molecule. The substructures contain functional groups that are related to a range of chemical properties and are potentially involved in downstream tasks related to the function of molecules. \citet{Bouritsas2023} developed graph substructure networks, a topologically aware message passing scheme based on substructure encoding. The method involves encoding structural roles based on the appearance of specific substructures and using them in message passing updates. Since message-passing graph neural networks (GNNs) are at most as powerful as the Weisfeiler--Lehman test, they cannot isolate sufficient features from the graph structure \citep{Bouritsas2023}. Graph substructure networks, on the other hand, capture these substructure-related features. Encoders pretrained on unlabeled molecules, whether graph-based \citep{kishimoto2023} or transformers on SMILES \citep{ross2022}, can also produce embeddings that capture structural features. 

\section{Models for molecular property prediction}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]

There are a wide variety of model architectures that can be used for QSAR modeling. Starting off with simpler and more explainable models, we have tree-based methods. Decision trees are non-parametric supervised learning algorithms that perform recursive binary partitioning of the feature space \citep{Breiman1984}. A decision tree builds a predictive model by learning a series of if-then rules that recursively split the data based on given feature values, creating a tree  where internal nodes represent decision rules, branches represent the results of those decisions, and leaf nodes contain predicted values or class labels \citep{Breiman1984}. Each recursive split is chosen to maximize  homogeneity within the resulting partitions. For classification, Gini impurity is commonly used and for regression, mean squared error reduction \citep{Breiman1984}.

There are several properties that make decision trees a good foundation for QSAR modeling. First, they handle non-linear relationships between molecular descriptors and target properties without requiring explicit feature engineering or transformation \citep{Breiman1984}. They also do not make assumptions about the underlying distribution of the data, making them robust to violations of parametric assumptions that can limit linear regression or other classical methods \citep{Svetnik2003}. 

Decision trees handle data in a ``contributory'' rather than ``consensus'' manner. In consensus methods such as linear regression or neural networks, every data point influences the prediction at every other point in the feature space. Tree-based methods make predictions based on the subset of training samples that fall into the same leaf node as the test point \citep{Breiman1984}. This mechanism essentially quarantines outliers in their respective leaf nodes, limiting their impact on predictions for samples in other regions or chemical domains. 

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: tree instability is from Breiman 1996 (bagging), not the Random Forests paper.
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: 'preventing systematic biases from propagating' is not in Breiman2001.
Individual decision trees tend to have high variance and often overfit, particularly at large depths \citep{Breiman1984}. Small modifications made to the training data can result in large differences in the structure of the tree, resulting in unstable predictions \citep{Breiman1996}. Ensemble methods are used to counteract this instability, aggregating predictions from many trees. Random forests (RFs) aggregate predictions from trees trained on bootstrap samples of data with random subsets of features at each split \citep{Breiman2001}. Bootstrapping ensures that each tree is trained on a slightly different subset of data, which effectively reduces the influence of any individual sample \citep{Breiman2001}. This is especially beneficial with noisy samples. The selection of random features at every node decorrelates the trees, which lowers the generalization error of the ensemble \citep{Breiman2001}. The aggregation of trees further prevents overfitting to noise by smoothing out prediction errors from individual noisy labels. 

Neural networks (NNs), a very popular family of models, can also be used for QSAR, although with mixed results. An NN is a system of interconnected nodes which learn by passing information through its layers. There are weights and biases attached to the nodes and various other parameters which the model optimizes to minimize the difference between the predicted output and the actual target value. During training, the network parameters are updated using backpropagation, in which gradients of the loss function with respect to each weight are propagated backward through the network \citep{Rumelhart1986}. The structure of these networks and the hyperparameters used depend on the type of NN. In QSAR, one of the most commonly used architectures is the multilayer perceptron (MLP), a NN containing fully-connected layers with non-linear activation functions which transforms molecular feature vectors (i.e., fingerprints, descriptors) into a property or activity prediction \citep{Ma2015}.

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: wu2018 (MoleculeNet) does not recommend 2-3 layers of 128-512 units; it lists the hyperparameters it searched. Citation and the specific numbers removed; if these are our own settings, say so.
A typical MLP for molecular property prediction has a few fully connected hidden layers with rectified linear unit (ReLU) activations and dropout regularization to prevent overfitting. Training is performed using backpropagation with gradient descent optimizers such as Adam, to minimize a loss function (mean squared error for regression, cross-entropy for classification) on batches of training data. 

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Koutsoukas2017 finds the reverse of 'NNs are often outperformed' (tuned DNNs outperform NB, kNN, RF, SVM); Baskin2008 is a review making no such comparison; neither says MLPs use 3D structure. Sentence on small datasets/3D removed: find a source if wanted.
However, NN performance on QSAR-related tasks has been very mixed. NNs have a long history in QSAR \citep{Baskin2008}, and tuned deep networks have outperformed shallow methods such as random forests and support vector machines across diverse activity classes \citep{Koutsoukas2017}. Their complexity and use of gradients make them prone to label memorization \citep{Zhang2017}, which in the context of noise is problematic, as it means that models may memorize the noise themselves. Although models that are memorizing labels may initially appear to have strong predictive performance, they cannot generalize to out-of-distribution samples properly and often provide inaccurate uncertainty estimates. Various approaches have been proposed to improve neural network robustness to label noise, including noise-robust loss functions \citep{Song2022}, confident learning to identify mislabeled examples \citep{Northcutt2021}, and regularization techniques such as mixup \citep{Zhang2018mixup} or label smoothing \citep{Szegedy2015}. 

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: the GCN is Kipf & Welling 2017; TeachOpenCADD and Xu2019 are later.
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: TeachOpenCADD does not say GINs suit activity cliffs.
All the models up to this point have assumed a vector-based input. Molecular graphs, on the other hand, require different types of predictive modeling. Two standard ML methods on molecular graphs include Graph Convolutional Networks (GCNs) and Graph Isomorphism Networks (GINs). GCNs apply the concept of convolution to graph-structured data. Instead of operating on grid-based data, such as images, GCNs aggregate information from neighbors of a node to update its representation \citep{Kipf2017}. In contrast, GINs address the limitations in GCNs’ ability to differentiate between non-isomorphic graphs. Unlike GCNs, which normalize and weight the sum of neighboring node features, GINs use a simple sum followed by a trainable MLP, giving the model flexibility to learn complex transformations of node features. The GIN model’s design is informed by the Weisfeiler--Lehman test for graph isomorphism, enabling it to distinguish between molecular graphs that are structurally similar but differ in subtle ways \citep{TeachOpenCADD2023, Xu2019}. GIN features have been competitive with or better than fingerprints and descriptors at classifying pairs of molecules as activity cliffs (ACs; Section~\ref{sec:mol_similarity}), where small variations in molecular structure can lead to significant changes in chemical or biological activity \citep{Dablander2023}. Descriptor-based models still predicted the potency of cliff molecules better than deep learning methods \citep{vanTilborg2022}. 


\subsection{Tree ensembles}

Gradient boosting methods such as eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) sequentially fit trees to the residuals of previous iterations, progressively reducing the prediction error \citep{Chen2016, Mustapha2016, Tian2022}. We used a handful of gradient-boosted tree ensembles in this thesis including Light Gradient-Boosting Machine (LightGBM) \citep{ke2017lightgbm}, and Natural Gradient Boosting (NGBoost) \citep{Duan2020}.

