No single method is expected to win, since averaged over all problems no learning algorithm outperforms another \citep{Wolpert1996}. \citet{Cortes2015} compared 12 algorithms on 12 bioactivity datasets with increasing Gaussian label noise; gradient boosting was the least tolerant, the other 11 degraded comparably, and two polynomial-kernel models became sensitive only at high noise. This is a recurring theme in LNL research: there is no universal solution to handling noise with experimental endpoints. Neighbor-based cleaning dates back to editing a training set by its nearest neighbors \citep{Wilson1972}, and it relies on similar molecules having similar labels, an assumption that activity cliffs break. QSAR models also frequently fail to predict activity cliffs \citep{Dablander2023}. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} tests the assumption that similar molecules have similar labels, and Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} tests neighbor-based noise mitigation with the hybrid as the feature space.


