\section{Introduction}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Deng2023's headline is RDKit2D descriptors, not ECFP; \cite as subject changed to \citet.
While there have been many investigations into the effect of noise on molecular property prediction, direction comparisons between how different molecular representations responds to label noise remains limited. The choice of molecular representation certainly affects predictive performance. \citet{Deng2023} evaluated 62,820 models and found that fixed representations, in most datasets RDKit 2D descriptors, outperformed representations learned from SMILES and molecular graphs. Whether these conclusions translate to noise robustness is our question. Between different types of label noise, \citet{Heid2023} found no difference in model performance between error distributions with the same mean and standard deviation. We expand on this by considering alternative patterns of noise, namely noise concentrated in structural groups or on a sparse subset of samples.


This chapter addresses the questions set out in Chapter~\ref{chap:intro}, specifically how the choice of representation and model architecture explain the variance in predictive performance and in noise robustness, what is the impact of the type of label noise, and whether uncertainty estimates track label noise.

