Traditional approaches load entire datasets into memory from CSV or Parquet files, creating RAM bottlenecks at scale and redundant deserialization overhead across experimental replicates. Our pipeline instead serializes preprocessed data into custom memory-mapped binary files (\texttt{.mmap}) that enable on-demand access with constant low memory footprint regardless of dataset size (Figure~\ref{fig:fig_mmap}). Python writes one file per split, the Rust program reads each file and writes a new one in which the processed labels replace the raw ones, and Python then reads the new files back for training.

