\section{Artificial noise conditions}

Many studies involving experimental label noise rely on the assumption that noise can be modeled by a Gaussian distribution. We decided to vary the distribution which noise is drawn from, as well as the methodology by which it's injected to specific labels. Five of the seven conditions draw each label's error on its own, so the error is random. Grouped-shifted gives a whole scaffold family a shared offset, and censoring pulls every label above a limit down to that limit, so in both the error is systematic. We controlled the quantity of noise by adding it in proportion to the spread of the clean labels on the training set, so a difference between two conditions is a difference in pattern rather than amount. One condition, censoring, could not be held to a set amount as its levels count the fraction of labels clipped.

We compare Gaussian noise on the HOMO--LUMO gap to Laplace, Student-$t$ and outlier noise in Figure~\ref{fig:deep}. The shape of the distribution from which the noise was drawn from made little difference. SVM reported higher \aucnorm{} values on non-Gaussian label noise, 0.937 under Gaussian noise against 0.947, 0.952 and 0.955 under Student-$t$, outlier and Laplace noise on PDV, while NGBoost lost \aucnorm{} under every one. NGBoost's relative stronger performance on Gaussian makes sense as it fits a per-molecule scale under a Gaussian likelihood, which the other distributions violate. SVM gains \aucnorm{} and NGBoost loses it under all three distributions across all datasets and representations. The size of the shift stays small, which is why these results coincide with \citet{Heid2023}, who found no difference between error distributions drawn at a matched mean and standard deviation. The shape of the noise does not change how much performance a model keeps, but it does change which model keeps the most.

While alternative shapes of noise may be accurately modeled by the Gaussian distribution, we do not find that is the case for systematic label noise. Relative to Gaussian, grouped-shifted noise lowered the \aucnorm{} of across all models and representations (Table~\ref{tab:condition_cost}). The models struggle to make predictions when the noise is isolated on molecules of specific scaffold families instead of being applied randomly across every sample. The variational networks retained the most predictive performance, while the plain, deterministic NNs lost the most. The predictive abilities of BNN-$\alpha$ degraded at a slower rate compared to its deterministic counterpart on every representation except PDV, while BNN-$\beta$ did so only on MHG-GNN and PDV. Grouped-wider noise changed no model beyond its replicate spread except QRF on Avalon and MHG-GNN, which makes predictions particularly well under this noise condition.  Censoring created the largest drop in predictive performance, \aucnorm{} fell by 0.12 to 0.18 for the same QSAR models relative to Gaussian noise (Table~\ref{tab:censoring}).

Most of these observations are being made at the upper end of the noise range. With ECFP4, six of the seven most robust
models start within 0.015 of one another in $R^2$ for clean labels, stay together up to a noise level of 0.5 and
separate only above 1.0, finishing between 0.692 for LightGBM and 0.741 for RF
(Figure~\ref{fig:curves}). We ranked the models by predictive performance at each noise level. Of the 13 base models, RF started ranked seventh, but by level 1.0 it was ranked first. SVM on the other hand falls from first to fifth (Figure~\ref{fig:rank_level}). Because \aucnorm{} weights the whole range from 0 to 1.5 equally, and the first half of it separates little, it has the potential to understate just how much the pattern of the noise costs. However, choosing to look at performance of a set level has its drawbacks, as the ranks shift at a different pace between noise conditions.

