These results follow from the loss function each model fits. NGBoost fits a per-molecule scale under a Gaussian likelihood, so it loses ground when the noise is not Gaussian. SVM ignores residuals that fall inside its margin, so it gains under the same conditions. QRF fits conditional quantiles, so a noisy scaffold family widens that family's interval without shifting the fit. Each boosting stage fits the residuals of the stage before it, and those residuals are largest in the noisy families. The GP is the most affected by error that is correlated with structure, because its kernel measures structure. None of this matters if the data are clean, as little separated the models below a noise level of 0.3.

In this chapter each model was given a standard molecular representation, and the choice of representation explained relatively little of the variance in noise robustness. We observe that this choice does impact the performance of different models to varying degrees, and the representations seem to capture different signals on each dataset that we tested. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} asks how well these representations compare molecules on a single dataset.

