\section{Introduction}

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Bajusz2015 does not say the choice matters little; Euclidean- and Manhattan-based similarities were not recommended.
The previous chapters have been focused on the impact of label noise. Before we delve into techniques that are meant to train on, identify, and mitigate such label noise, we first focus on a tangential point, molecular similarity. All of the aforementioned noise-adjacent methods, as well as the QSAR modeling itself, rest on the similar property principle (SPP; Section~\ref{sec:mol_similarity}), which posits that structurally similar compounds display similar chemical properties \citep{Johnson1990}. This serves as the conceptual foundation for QSAR modeling. The concept of molecular similarity is also used in various contexts to compare neighboring molecules. This comes into play when identifying noisy molecules that in theory stick out from their neighbors. There are some important exceptions to the SPP, namely activity cliffs \citep{Stumpfe2012, vanTilborg2022}. Furthermore, there is no single way to define similarity between compounds. This is where the principle begins to break down in practice. When defining similarity between two molecules, we typically rely on some pairwise mathematical function, or coefficient, that takes in two molecular representations as input. Two factors vary here, the representation and the coefficient. \citet{Bajusz2015} compared eight coefficients and found Tanimoto, Dice, Cosine and Soergel the best and largely equivalent, while similarities derived from Euclidean and Manhattan distances were not recommended on their own. In this chapter we ask if the standard methods of comparing molecules satisfy the SPP, insofar as they do not sufficiently quantify how similar two molecules actually are.

General-purpose molecular representations are designed to work across chemical domains. Consequently, when considering data from a specific domain, many of their features show little to no variance. Some representations assign separate molecules identical feature vectors. Since common fingerprints do not encode stereochemistry \citep{orsi2024map4c}, they are often unable to differentiate stereoisomers, which can have substantially different measured activity \citep{Nguyen2006}. As these coefficients compare every feature, irrelevant features dilute the result. Different representations also disagree about which molecules are close based on the information they carry. 

The problem of whether neighboring molecules can predict chemical activity has been measured before. The modelability index of \citet{Golbraikh2014} scores a binary dataset by the proportion of nearest-neighbor pairs that share an activity class, producing a single value for how strongly the similarity assumption holds on that particular combination of representation and data. It is not clear whether a low value is due to the data being intrinsically hard to model or to the representation not capturing adequate features for the task. Other work in this area relates to activity cliffs. \citet{Sheridan2020} compared experimental error, activity distribution and activity-cliff metrics as predictors of how well a dataset can be modeled, and found the cliff metrics the strongest of the three. However, they found that these metrics cannot separate real cliffs from those produced by uncertain activity measurements. \citet{MedinaFranco2013} point out that some of these activity cliffs are artifacts of the chosen representation, similarity threshold, or noise in the data. Activity cliffs are distinct from noise, as they carry some of the most valuable information about the interaction between structure and activity. None of these measures ask specifically about whether the specific features within these representations matter for the property at hand. 


% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Riniker2013 used 88 targets from 3 collections; what correlated was screening performance; the 2 baselines are in addition to the 12.
We first consider how much the representation impacts similarity measures. \citet{Riniker2013} benchmarked 12 fingerprints and two simple baseline fingerprints for similarity-based virtual screening on 88 targets from three public collections, and found that the screening performance of the 12 fingerprints was highly correlated. Only the two baselines differed significantly from the rest. Neural embeddings were found to have a negligible improvement over ECFP across 25 pretrained models and 25 datasets \citep{Praski2025}. However, when looking into the differences between the structure of fingerprints, \citet{Huber2026} found that fingerprints which store raw counts are more effective at defining similarity than folded fingerprints when compared against a graph-based structural reference. Folding introduces bit collisions, which disproportionately distort similarity for molecules with many set bits \citep{Huber2026}. ACs follow the same pattern: across 15 configurations of representation and similarity measures on 3 datasets, the representations disagreed about which pairs form cliffs \citep{DabrowskiTumanski2026}, supporting the point \citet{MedinaFranco2013} make about ACs being artifacts of their representation. 

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Wilson1972 removes disagreeing samples; it does not smooth labels.
Any method that incorporates similarity measures, such as AC detection, or cleaning a training set by comparing each sample's label with its nearest neighbors' labels, whether by removing the samples that disagree \citep{Wilson1972} or by smoothing their labels, inherits this choice, as do the noise estimation and mitigation methods of this thesis. \citet{Coupry2022} learn a molecular similarity from graph embeddings, but independently of any endpoint. \citet{Zheng2000} did choose the similarity for the task. They predict each molecule's activity as the average over its $k$ most similar molecules by Euclidean distance over descriptors, and choose the descriptors by simulated annealing to maximize the leave-one-out $q^2$. \citet{Itskowitz2005} also optimize the number of neighbors and their weighting. Both select features for a nearest-neighbor model of one endpoint, whereas this thesis selects features across several representations.

The purpose of this chapter is to establish the problem that Chapter~\ref{chap:method} sets out to solve. Section~\ref{sec:flaws:representations} asks, for a given dataset, how much the features of standard representations vary and how many molecules map to identical feature vectors. Section~\ref{sec:flaws:similarity} asks what happens when these features are used to quantify similarity, and Section~\ref{sec:flaws:consequences} follows the consequences for methods built on these similarity measures, including a small sweep of published noise-handling methods (Section~\ref{sec:lnl_motivation}).


\section{Flaws in standard representations}\label{sec:flaws:representations}

General-purpose representations are built to span a broad range of chemical domains. When your dataset covers only a small region of the chemical space, a lot of the features within these generalized representations become irrelevant, describing chemistry which is absent from the data at hand. Pairwise comparisons of these representations rely on these irrelevant features.


\subsection{Constant and near-constant features}\label{sec:flaws:constant} 

To investigate similarity estimation, we first look at a simple measure: how many features vary within a dataset. A single dataset often contains a set of relatively similar molecules. Many features within a generalized representation contain information that describes all, or almost all, of the molecules in the set, failing to capture the variation between them. We define a constant feature to be one that takes a single value in every molecule of a dataset and a near-constant feature to be one that carries the same value in at least 95\% of the molecules. We counted constant and near-constant features from a set of representations (described in Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}) across hERG $K_i$, Caco-2, LogD, QM9, MDR1--MDCK efflux, human liver microsome (HLM) clearance, rat liver microsome (RLM) clearance and the Lhasa hERG blocker training set. HLM clearance and the Lhasa hERG set are not in Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}; their base representations are those with counts in Table~\ref{tab:constant_features_b}.

The substructure-based fingerprints have the most near-constant features. The learned embeddings, which are taken from the last layers of a NN, have none except MHG-GNN (Tables~\ref{tab:constant_features} and~\ref{tab:constant_features_b}, Figure~\ref{fig:constant_features}). ECFP4 is the worst offender. On hERG $K_i$ (1,412 molecules) 1,815 of the 2,048 features are near-constant. That jumps to 1,997 out of 2,048 near-constant features for the HOMO--LUMO gap on QM9. 292 of MHG-GNN's 1,024 features are zero in every molecule across all seven datasets that used it. They come directly from the saved encoder output rather than the data, and we have not looked into why. 

