We counted these ties on hERG $K_i$, LogD and Caco-2, for each dataset's base representations and augmentation features (small feature sets added to the hybrid after it is built, Chapter~\ref{chap:method}, ``Augmentation''). Two molecules are tied when a representation gives them exactly the same feature vector. No tie came from one compound listed twice (checked by InChIKey) or from a row of zeros, so every tie merges two different molecules (Table~\ref{tab:flaws:ties}).

Apart from the MACCS keys, most tied molecules are stereoisomers. Atom pairs, PDV, Mordred and SMI-TED tie only stereoisomers, and ECFP4, Avalon and topological torsions add a few molecules that differ in more than their stereochemistry. On hERG $K_i$, ECFP4, atom pairs, PDV, Mordred and SMI-TED tie the same 56 molecules, so joining any of these five representations leaves all 56 tied. The WL graph kernel also ties 49 hERG $K_i$ and 26 Caco-2 molecules that are not stereoisomers, and the MACCS keys tie the most molecules on every dataset, mostly not stereoisomers. The 3D augmentation features and the learned embeddings ChemBERT, GROVER, MHG-GNN, MoLFormer and GraphMVP tie none, and joining all of a dataset's representations leaves no molecule tied. % CHECK: the ChemBERT count comes from the cached chembert.npy (256 features). Whether that cache matches the current featurizer code is unverified.

A model has to give every molecule in a tied group the same prediction. The best single prediction for a group is its mean label, so ties set an error that no model can go below,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{RMSE}_{\min} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{G}\sum_{i \in G}\left(y_i - \bar{y}_G\right)^2},
\end{equation}
where $n$ is the number of molecules, $G$ runs over the tied groups and $\bar{y}_G$ is the mean label of group $G$. The matching best $R^2$ is $1 - \mathrm{RMSE}_{\min}^2 / \mathrm{Var}(y)$. On hERG $K_i$ this lowest error is at most $0.134$ p$K_i$ (MACCS keys), small next to the label standard deviation of $0.915$ p$K_i$.

