The study used one dataset, one property and one noise condition (Gaussian noise on every training label), and Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} repeats the question on more datasets and under other noise conditions (Section~\ref{sec:noise_injection}). It also added the distance step to methods designed without one, so poor adaptation is an alternative explanation.

The distances may have failed for two reasons, which the evidence here does not separate. First, a single representation may lack the information a distance needs, with and possibly even without noise. PDVs encode global molecular properties, ECFP4 contains substructure information and MHG-GNNs encode the molecular graph \citep{kishimoto2023}, and any one of these sources alone may not be sufficient for robust similarity estimation. Second, some representations are sparse, with many features irrelevant to the molecules of a given dataset (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:similarity}). We sought to address the second with the adaptive hybrid representation (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}), which concatenates the top-ranked features by importance from several base representations.
