Neighbor-based noise detection treats a label far from its neighbors' labels as probably wrong, an idea that goes back to editing nearest-neighbor training sets \citep{Wilson1972}. The wider literature on filtering noisy labels concerns classification \citep{Brodley1999, frenay2014classification}. \citet{Martin2021} adapt fourteen classification noise filters to regression, seven of them nearest-neighbor filters that use the neighbors' mean label. They find that the filters improve regression methods on 20 general regression benchmarks with injected label noise, none of them QSAR datasets.
