For each representation $k$, the importance method gives every feature a score $s^{(k)}_j \ge 0$, computed on the 80\% part of the internal split (Figure~\ref{fig:split_levels}). These scores rank features only within their own base representation. The standalone score $r_k$ is the score of the internal evaluator, trained on representation $k$ in the 80\% part and scored on the 20\% part, in the dataset's metric. These scores cannot be compared across metrics as they stand: a lower RMSE is better and it has no fixed upper limit, whereas a Spearman correlation is higher when better and lies between $-1$ and 1. Before the budget is split, each $r_k$ is therefore converted to a score $\tilde r_k$ on which 0 means the representation predicts no better than a trivial predictor and 1 means it predicts perfectly. The trivial predictor depends on the metric. For Spearman, Pearson, the Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC) and $R^2$, $\tilde r_k = \max(0, r_k)$. For AUROC and the concordance index, $\tilde r_k = \max(0, 2(r_k - 0.5))$. For RMSE, $\tilde r_k = \max(0, 1 - \mathrm{RMSE}_k/\mathrm{RMSE}_0)$, where $\mathrm{RMSE}_0$ is the error of predicting the training mean, and mean absolute error is rescaled likewise against the training median. For accuracy and weighted F1, $\tilde r_k = \max(0, (r_k - r_0)/(1 - r_0))$, where $r_0$ is the score of always predicting the majority training class.

An optional representation count $N$ keeps the $N$ representations with the highest standalone scores on the fold and discards the rest for that iteration of the search. Each remaining representation receives a weight $w_k = g(\tilde r_k)$, with $g$ set by the allocation method, and a share $b^*_k = B\, w_k / \sum_l w_l$ features, rounded by largest remainder and capped at its width. A representation left out by the representation count, or whose standalone fit fails, receives no features. Thus $\sum_k b_k = \min(B, \sum_k d_k)$ and $b_k \le d_k$, and a representation with $w_k > 0$ keeps at least one feature. The global pool does not use these shares (see below).

Let $S_k$ be the top $b_k$ features of representation $k$ by $s^{(k)}_j$, among those with $s^{(k)}_j > 0$; if none scores above 0, its full $b_k$ is taken. The cut exists because tree importance gives exactly 0 to every feature no tree split on, and filling the budget with those would pick them by their position in the matrix. The hybrid is $\mathbf{H} = [\mathbf{X}^{(1)}_{:,S_1}, \dots, \mathbf{X}^{(K)}_{:,S_K}]$, with $\sum_k |S_k| \le B$, so it can be narrower than its budget.

The hybrid search returns a list of settings for feature selection, referred to here as a configuration. It is scored by fitting the downstream model on the hybrid in each fold of the search and averaging the held-out scores. Each pair of downstream model and budget has its own search, and thus its own winner. Because this score is chosen over a range of selected configurations, it is optimistic \citep{Varma2006, Cawley2010}. However, so is its direct comparison with the highest-performing base representation on the dataset. The whole search runs inside each outer training fold, and the winning settings are rebuilt there and scored once on the untouched outer test fold, both for the winner at each budget and for each downstream model's best across budgets.
