\paragraph{Ensembles.}
% TODO: manual review -- kept from the 2026-10-08 text (c11_ensemble_vs_hybrid.py), shortened; the two ensembles reported are ensemble_s1_auto and ensemble_s2_auto
The ensemble fits one model per representation and pools their predictions (late fusion), whereas the hybrid fits one model on one fused feature vector (early fusion) \citep{Stahlschmidt2022}. Every choice for the ensemble is made on an inner 3-fold group split of the outer training samples. The first ensemble uses the same downstream model on every representation, and the second uses each representation's own better model. Each picks its members, the top 3 representations or all of them, and one of seven ways to pool predictions, from the mean and median to trimmed, outlier-removed and score-weighted means. We also record the number of representations and models needed at prediction time.

\paragraph{Many or few representations.}
% TODO: manual review -- [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] new paragraph from tests/experiments/complexity/c18_diversity_vs_depth.py (docstring 4-26; ranking and depth arms :480-500)
This experiment asks whether, at a fixed budget $B$, features are better drawn from many representations or from few. On each outer fold, the base representations are ranked by their summed feature importance on the training samples. The top 1, 2 or 3 of them supply the $B$ features with the highest importance among their own features. These are compared with the search's own hybrid at $B$ and with an even split of $B$ over every base representation. Every arm uses the importance method that the fold's best hybrid used. The search's hybrid differs from the hand-built arms in two ways at once: it ranks representations by their standalone scores rather than summed importance, and it stops at the last feature with importance above zero.

