Federated learning trains one model across data held by several parties without moving the data \citep{McMahan2017}, and it has been run across pharmaceutical companies \citep{Heyndrickx2024}, whose structures and assay results are commercial assets that cannot be pooled \citep{Hanser2023,Bassani2023}. Our collaborator, Lhasa Limited, uses the teacher-student process of Chapter~\ref{chap:background} (Table~\ref{tab:noise_sources}) \citep{Hanser2025}. We use their code for the fingerprints, site models and site clustering, and re-implement from it the step that combines the site models' predictions.

% TODO: manual review -- kept from the 2026-10-08 text; the sentences about the hERG comparison (no student, class balancing) removed with that design
The site models' predictions are combined in one of two ways. Under distance weighting, each site's vote on a molecule is weighted by how close the molecule is to that site's training molecules, so a site counts most on chemistry it has seen. The distance is measured to the 8 nearest molecules in a random 20\% sample of that site's training molecules, on the features the site model uses. Distances are Manhattan by default, whereas the collaborator's code uses Euclidean. Under score weighting, each site has one weight, its own cross-validated score. Only predictions on shared molecules cross a site boundary. The published process then trains a student model on the most confident combined labels.

% TODO: manual review -- kept from the 2026-10-08 text (fed03_synthetic_private.py:427-450, 1208-1240; federated_helpers.py:1577-1624)
Our addition is that feature selection, as well as model fitting, takes place at the sites. One search on the training molecules of all sites together chooses the settings (allocation method, importance method and internal evaluator). Each site then selects features with these settings on its own molecules and reports only feature indices, importance values and one score. A coordinator splits the total budget $B$ across sites in proportion to their scores, with at least one feature for each site whose selection succeeded. Only this proportional split is run, so we cannot say what the site scores buy. A feature already taken by another site is replaced by the site's next-ranked feature, so all features in the hybrid are distinct. Indices and importances are smaller, and harder to invert, than model weights or gradients. In related work, each client runs mutual-information feature selection on its own data and a server aggregates the results \citep{Cassara2022}.

% TODO: manual review -- [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] new paragraph replacing the hERG design. From tests/experiments/federated/fed03_synthetic_private.py (REP_FAMILIES :87-110; C3 same_width_as_c2 notes :2150-2165) and the result files (config.silo_reps, budgets, headline_budget 500, 3 seeds). Caco-2 families: structural = atom_pair, avalon, ecfp4, graph_kernel, top_torsion; physicochemical = pdv; learned = grover, mhg_gnn, molformer, smited.
All four separate-site experiments run on Caco-2 and LogD, with five scaffold-group folds and three seeds, the DNN and SVM, and budgets of 500 to 2{,}000 features. In the main one, each site holds the same molecules but one family of representations: substructure fingerprints, physicochemical descriptors or pretrained embeddings. It compares the best single family, picked on the training molecules, with one feature selection over all families, and with one selection per family at an even share of the budget. When a family is narrower than its share, the rest goes to the other families, so the even split can build fewer features than one selection over all families. The built widths are recorded on every fold.

% TODO: manual review -- [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] rewritten from fed01_noise_heterogeneity.py (docstring; K=5 :78; flip levels :79; two-class cutoffs federated_helpers.py CLASSIFICATION_CUTOFFS: Caco-2 log10 efflux ratio >= log10 2, LogD >= 3), fed02_rep_diversity_silos.py (config.silo_reps) and fed04_cluster_aware_importance.py. The defect in the arm that removes flagged molecules is stated here (silo_failures in caco2__folds.partial.jsonl).
Three further experiments are run. In the first, labels are made two-class (an efflux ratio of at least 2 on Caco-2, a LogD of at least 3), and five sites are formed by $k$-means clusters of the molecules. Up to 30\% of each site's training labels are flipped, at different rates on different sites. Selection at the sites is compared, at each flip level, with the ensembles and the student model of the published process, with one model on the pooled molecules, and with the same selection made on all molecules pooled. In one version, each site first removes the molecules it flags as noisy. Some sites then hold too few molecules, groups or classes to score a representation, and their selection is refused, so this version differs from the plain one in two ways at once. In the second experiment, each of five sites holds the same molecules as a different representation, and one hybrid over all sites is compared with the best single site and with the published ensembles. In the third, the molecules are split into $k$-means clusters, and features are selected per cluster and used for a model on all molecules, or used for a separate model per cluster. It also asks whether the clusters agree on which representations matter, against random subsets of the same size. The first and third experiments report MCC on the two-class labels, and the second reports RMSE.

% TODO: manual review -- kept from the 2026-10-08 text, first sentence changed: the comparison no longer uses a proprietary source
The separate-site work has three limitations. The sites are simulated from one public dataset, so the differences between real sites in curation and chemistry are not tested. It depends on the collaborator's implementation of the published process. The published process trains a student on a large separate public set. Here the training molecules take that role as a compromise, and held-out molecules are excluded from it.



