The gain is not a matter of the hybrid beating a poorly chosen representation. Every representation in each pool was also scored on its own on the same folds, so we know which single representation was best on the held-out molecules, a choice no user could make in advance. On every small-molecule dataset the hybrid beats that representation too, with both models. On Caco-2 with the DNN the best single representation in hindsight is PDV, at 0.2980 RMSE, against 0.2775 for the hybrid. The reason lies in what the hybrid draws on. When each representation's features are shuffled in turn on the held-out molecules, the score of the hybrid on hERG $K_i$, Caco-2 and MDR1--MDCK efflux drops through five to seven representations about equally, and no single representation carries more than 29\% of it. On hERG $K_i$ with the SVM the effective number of representations behind the prediction is 7.17 of 10. Fingerprints, descriptors, pretrained graph encoders and SMILES language models each hold part of the signal, and no one of them holds all of it. This is the premise of the thesis: a single representation leaves information on the table, and selecting from several recovers it. On QM9 with LightGBM and on hERG blocking the hybrid leans more on one representation, which carries 88\% and about half of its score, and it still beats every single representation.

