The search scores its own pick on the training samples, so that score is optimistic by an amount we can measure: the search's score minus the held-out score of the same pick, with budgets searched up to 8,000 features. On the larger small-molecule datasets the difference is small. On LogD it is 0.0029 RMSE with the DNN and 0.0013 with the SVM, and on Caco-2 it is within 0.001 either way. On the small antibody datasets the search promises much more than it delivers: 0.1571 Spearman on colloidal stability with the DNN, 0.1366 on binding free energy with the SVM and 0.1222 on expression titer with the random forest. On these datasets the folds also often pick a hybrid wider than their own training set, for example on every fold of EGFR binding with the SVM. This is the same divide as in the comparison with single representations. With a few thousand molecules the search's internal scores are reliable enough to choose between configurations, and with 150 to 500 training antibodies they are not, so the choice it makes is partly noise.

