% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Cawley2010 does not argue that feature selection is model selection with a complexity cost.
\citet{Ambroise2002} showed the extreme case of overfitting through feature selection. On colon-cancer data with permuted class labels, an SVM with recursive feature elimination reached a cross-validated error close to zero when genes were selected on the full dataset first. With selection redone inside each fold, the error was between 0.40 and 0.45, close to chance. The bias from pre-selection can exceed that from hyperparameter tuning \citep{Vabalas2019}, and over-fitting the model-selection criterion biases performance estimates in the same way \citep{Cawley2010}. The hybrid search computes all importance scores within each fold, on training data only, and every number in this chapter is scored on molecules the search never saw, so the gains above are not this bias. The bias still exists inside the search, and the numbers above show where it is large: on small datasets, where many configurations are compared on few molecules \citep{Tibshirani2015}.

