Repeating each fold's search with one more fold of training samples left out makes the held-out score worse on every small-molecule dataset with both models, by 0.009 to 0.037 RMSE. On LogD with the SVM, for example, it is worse by 0.0295 on average over the five held-out folds. The two searches differ in two ways at once, the training samples and the budget each one picks, so this says that the hybrid gains from more data, not which of the two changes causes it. Together with the selection bias above, it points the same way: the hybrid is a method that needs data, and gets better with more of it.

