The hybrid's gain is not only the gain of having fewer features. At the same number of features, drawn from all base representations joined, we compared it with recursive feature elimination and with features drawn at random. On QM9, Caco-2, LogD and MDR1--MDCK efflux the hybrid has the lowest error at every budget with both models. On LogD with the SVM at 1,000 features, for example, it reaches 0.4303 RMSE against 0.4452 for recursive elimination and 0.4745 for random features. Recursive elimination ranks every feature of the joined representations in one list with one forest, which is the pooled ranking the hybrid avoids (Section~\ref{sec:method:searchspace}): features from a 2,048-bit fingerprint, a descriptor vector and a learned embedding are put on one scale. The hybrid ranks features within each representation first and then decides how much of each to keep, and on small molecules that is better. hERG $K_i$ with the DNN is the exception, where recursive elimination (0.5794) and even random features (0.5705) beat the hybrid (0.6112) at 1,000 features. On antibodies random features are beaten at most budgets, but recursive elimination is ahead on binding free energy with the SVM and on CHO polyreactivity at every budget. mRMR stops at 200 features, so it ran only on the three antibody datasets whose grids include 50 or 100 features.

