\section{Conclusions}
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] written from the results of this chapter (outline 5.11: one paragraph per research question, then the bridge). Questions from Section sec:intro:hybrid_questions.

% TODO: manual review
This chapter asked how the best set of features can be selected for a given prediction task (Section~\ref{sec:intro:hybrid_questions}). The answer is that it depends on the dataset and the downstream model, and that a search can find it. The adaptive hybrid representation selects features from up to ten base representations within each fold, with the importance method, allocation method, internal evaluator, number of representations and budget all searched. On every small-molecule dataset it beats the best single representation with both downstream models, on every fold of the six regression datasets, and it beats even the representation that was best in hindsight. It does so because the representations carry different information: on hERG $K_i$, Caco-2 and MDR1--MDCK efflux the hybrids draw on five to seven of them, and no one holds more than 29\% of the signal. That was the hypothesis behind building the hybrid, that the feature set is part of the problem, and on small molecules it holds.

% TODO: manual review
The gain comes from selection, not only from having fewer features. At the same number of features the hybrid beats recursive elimination and random features on four of the five small-molecule datasets with a result, and drawing on several representations beats drawing on the top one alone on 15 of 18 datasets. How the budget is split matters less than which representations are drawn on. No single setting suits every dataset and model: the DNN wants more features than the SVM, the number of representations ranges from two to all ten, and the cheaper internal evaluators are good enough on small molecules but not on some antibody datasets. The selected features can also be read: on LogD the hybrid picks lipophilicity and basicity on every fold, from two different representations.

% TODO: manual review -- the antibody sentence depends on the author's decision on the antibody paragraph in Section sec:method:results_single.
On antibodies the hybrid helps where the pool offers representations of different kinds or there are enough antibodies to choose well: on binding free energy, AC-SINS self-association and the 4,275-variant expression scan it beats every single representation. On the smaller sets its gains are inconsistent, the search's own scores are optimistic, and an ensemble of one model per representation often does better. The method also runs on reactions, where it gains nothing on the additive split, on data held at separate sites, where selecting at each site does as well as one selection over all representations, and with new embeddings added to the pool.

