\section{Introduction}

% TODO: manual review
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] first two sentences reuse the first paragraph of the old introduction.
Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} showed where the similarity assumption fails on standard representations. In most of them, the distance between two molecules barely rises with the difference in their activity. Any method that trusts a molecule's neighbors inherits this problem, from predicting a molecule's activity from its nearest neighbors to finding a noisy label by comparing it with its neighbors' labels. Chapter~\ref{chap:method} introduced the adaptive hybrid representation, a set of features selected from several base representations for one task. The original idea behind it was simple: select the features relevant to the task first, then compute distances on those features alone. This chapter returns to that idea.

% TODO: manual review
% Two research questions: THESIS_OUTLINE.md 6.1 ("two now rather than three"). The old third question, on noise
% mitigation and noise-robust training, moved to Chapter 7.
There are two ways to use a hybrid for similarity. In the first, the search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method} is run with a nearest-neighbor model as the downstream model, and distances are measured in the hybrid it selects. In the second, the search ranks each candidate hybrid by how well each molecule's activity is predicted from its five nearest neighbors in it, and no downstream model is fitted. We ask two research questions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Does distance in a hybrid track activity better than distance in the best single representation?
    \item Do the methods built on that distance work better in a hybrid? We test prediction from nearest neighbors, activity cliffs, Bayesian optimization, distance to the training molecules as a guide to prediction error, retrieval of neighbors and clustering.
\end{enumerate}

