The search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method} can be written as follows. Each configuration $\theta$ fixes an importance method, an allocation method, a number of representations and a budget $B$. Given base representations $R_1, \dots, R_m$, a configuration builds a feature set $S_\theta \subset R_1 \cup \dots \cup R_m$ with $|S_\theta| \le B$. The search returns $S_{\theta^*}$, where $\theta^* = \arg\max_\theta s(S_\theta)$ and $s$ is the search's score. In the general search, $s$ is a downstream model's score on the search's own held-out folds. In the similarity search, $s(S) = q_5(S)$, how accurately each molecule's activity is predicted from its five nearest neighbors in $S$ (Equation~\ref{eq:similarity_objective}). No downstream model is fitted. Everything else is shared with the general search: importance methods, allocation methods, numbers of representations, budgets and folds. The neighbor score, computed on a validation set taken from the fold's training molecules, also replaces the internal evaluator when the budget is split.

% TODO: manual review
This modifies a published approach. \citet{Zheng2000} and \citet{Itskowitz2005} chose descriptors to maximize nearest-neighbor prediction within one descriptor set. We extend this by choosing features across several representations, through the search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method}. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} showed that the representation decides which molecules count as neighbors. The similarity hybrid tests whether choosing features for the quality of the neighbors tackles this more directly than choosing them for prediction.

