To rank whole candidates, we compute the score on the held-out molecules of each fold of the search, inside the outer training fold, and these molecules act as each other's neighbors. Their groups (scaffolds, for molecules) are split into up to 5 parts, and each molecule's 5 nearest neighbors are drawn from the other parts. A neighbor with the same scaffold is an easy match and would inflate the score. This is the only place in the chapter where a molecule's own group is kept out of its neighbors. Each neighbor is weighted by the inverse of its distance \citep{Dudani1976}:
\begin{equation}
\hat{y}_i = \frac{\sum_{j\in N_5(i)} y_j / d_{ij}}{\sum_{j\in N_5(i)} 1/d_{ij}}.
\label{eq:similarity_objective}
\end{equation}
% TODO: manual review
A neighbor at distance 0 receives all of the weight, shared equally if there are several. On regression data, the score $q_5$ is the Spearman correlation $\rho(\hat{\mathbf{y}}, \mathbf{y})$. On binary data, $\hat{y}_i$ is the distance-weighted fraction of neighbors in class 1, and $q_5 = 2(\mathrm{AUROC} - 0.5)$, where AUROC is the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve. Both scores put chance at 0. The budget split needs this, because each representation's share of the budget is its score divided by the sum of the scores. The modelability index of \citet{Golbraikh2014} (Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}) is a related published measure. It uses a single nearest neighbor and a hard class match, whereas our score keeps the graded fraction of neighbors.

