Every experiment that predicts from neighbors or measures retrieval scores each feature space at every combination of the settings in Table~\ref{tab:knn_settings}, instead of at one chosen setting. A held-out molecule's prediction is the weighted mean of its neighbors' labels,
\begin{equation}
\hat{y}_i = \frac{\sum_{j \in N_k(i)} w_{ij}\, y_j}{\sum_{j \in N_k(i)} w_{ij}},
\label{eq:knn_weights}
\end{equation}
where $N_k(i)$ is the $k$ training molecules nearest to $i$ under distance $d$, and $w_{ij}$ is the neighbor weight. On classification data, each class receives the summed weight of its neighbors. A molecule's own scaffold group is never excluded from its neighbors, only the molecule itself. The one experiment that does not use these settings is the one on all representations joined (Section~\ref{sec:similarity:experiments}).

