An activity cliff is a pair of structurally similar molecules with very different activity \citep{Stumpfe2012,vanTilborg2022}. We decide which molecules are cliff molecules in each outer fold, from its training molecules. We keep the structural side of the rule of \citet{Hu2015}: two molecules are similar when their ECFP4 Tanimoto similarity is at least 0.55. We modify its activity side. A gap of two log units flags no molecule at all on Caco-2, so instead we solve for the gap that puts 15\% of the training molecules in a similar pair whose labels differ by more than it. On classification data, a similar pair counts when its two molecules are in different classes. A held-out molecule is a cliff molecule when it has at least two similar training molecules whose labels span more than that gap. Its own label is never read. Each downstream model is fitted on its two best single representations and its two best hybrids. Each fit is scored separately on the cliff molecules and on the other held-out molecules. The main comparison is the first hybrid against the best single representation.

\paragraph{Distance against prediction error.}
