Retrieval measures how close in activity a molecule's neighbors are. For each held-out molecule, the label gap is the weighted mean absolute difference between its label and those of its nearest training molecules, at every setting of Table~\ref{tab:knn_settings}. On classification data, it is one minus the weighted share of neighbors in the molecule's class. Lower is better. For clustering, we use k-means \citep{Lloyd1982} as implemented in scikit-learn. We fit it on the training molecules with 3, 5 and 10 clusters, and assign each held-out molecule to a cluster. Label coherence is one minus the mean standard deviation of the held-out labels within a cluster, divided by the standard deviation of all held-out labels. On classification data, it is the mean share of the largest class in each cluster. Higher is better. Clusters with fewer than two held-out molecules are left out.

