To find the best number of features, we take each downstream model's top hybrid at every budget of its search. At each budget we record, for that same hybrid, its prediction score and the correlation of distance against activity, and find the budget where each peaks. On hERG $K_i$ and Buchwald--Hartwig only, we simulate choosing which molecules to measure. Starting from a set of whole scaffold groups holding at least 5\% of the molecules, a surrogate model picks 100 molecules one at a time by expected improvement \citep{Jones1998}. We repeat this from three starting sets, and the score is the area under the best-found-so-far curve. Last, for each representation a predictive hybrid draws from, we compare the label information in the features it kept against a random subset of the same size. Each block of features is reduced to at most 50 principal components. A mutual-information estimate is summed over the components, and the mean over three label permutations is subtracted. We use scikit-learn's estimators, which follow \citet{Kraskov2004} for continuous labels and \citet{Ross2014} for classes. This sum counts information shared by two components twice.



