Three settings involve no injection. On the labels as delivered, we do not know which labels are wrong, so a method can only be judged by the held-out score. On hERG IC$_{50}$, many molecules were measured more than once, and the disagreement between those measurements is real noise. On QM9 with no noise added, the labels are clean. Three experiments also use damage of their own. The experiment on break-even precision gives a random 5\% of molecules wide errors and leaves every other label alone. The experiment that changes the model adds Gaussian noise sized by a LightGBM model's error. The experiment on measuring sources simulates several measurements per molecule. Each is described where it appears.

\subsection{Noise detection}\label{sec:noise:detection}

% TODO: manual review
A detector gives every training molecule a suspicion score, a number that is high when the label looks wrong (Table~\ref{tab:noise_terms}). It changes no label and fits no final model. We group the detectors by what they read (Table~\ref{tab:noise:detectors}). The first kind compares a molecule's label with a model's prediction for it, made without that molecule. The second compares it with its neighbors' labels. The third reads a model's own predicted spread for the molecule. Every detector of the first two kinds reads the molecule's own label. Three of the third kind, the heteroscedastic GP, the variance-head network and NGBoost, predict a spread from the features alone and never read it. That matters. A detector that never sees a label can only find a wrong one if the damage is tied to structure. Most detectors only rank molecules. A few of the third kind return a spread in the label's own units, which can feed a weight or a move.

