Two experiments change the space itself. The first multiplies each z-scored feature by the square root of its importance, rescaled so that the multipliers average 1, before the neighbors are found. The importance is computed against the damaged training labels, as feature-weighted nearest neighbors do \citep{Wettschereck1997}. The second compares four families of distance for the neighbor detectors. They are the plain features under Euclidean and Manhattan distance, the features weighted by importance raised to 0.5 or 1.0, forest proximity, and the hybrid's own features. Forest proximity between two molecules is one minus the share of trees in which they fall in the same leaf \citep{Breiman2001}. The forest has 100 trees with at least 5 molecules per leaf, fitted to the fold's damaged training labels.

