A detector can be graded only where the damage moved some labels and not others, so that it is known which molecules were damaged. That is outlier noise, censoring, the two shifts in 10\% and every label flip. Under Gaussian and grouped-shifted noise every label moves, and no detection score exists. We grade a detector by the average precision of its suspicion scores against the set of damaged molecules. A detector that ranks at random scores the damaged share of the training molecules, so that share is the chance level. Some experiments report the area under the ROC curve instead, where chance is 0.5.


\subsection{Noise mitigation}\label{sec:noise:mitigation}

% TODO: manual review
A noise mitigation method has two halves (Table~\ref{tab:noise_terms}). Something selects the molecules: a detector, a known flag or every molecule. Then one of three actions follows: delete the molecule, make it count less, or move its label. We describe each method by four things: what selects the molecules, how many it acts on, the action, and for a move, the target and how far the label moves (Table~\ref{tab:noise:mitigation}). Written this way, the many methods in the code reduce to a few selectors crossed with three actions, and the many ways of moving a label differ only in where the label moves to and how far.

% TODO: manual review
% SOURCE: src/kirby/utils/noise_estimation.py:2103-2109 (smooth_by_scores), 2178-2206 (weights_from_scores); n07_remediation.py:214, 957-998, 1049-1101, 1103-1168, 1247-1264
Most methods read the suspicion score rescaled to $[0, 1]$, $\tilde s_i = (s_i - \min s)/(\max s - \min s)$. Deleting drops the molecules above a quantile of $s$, usually the top 5\%, 10\% or 20\%. This is the filtering of \citet{Brodley1999}, which \citet{Zhao2017} tested on QSAR modeling sets. Down-weighting gives molecule $i$ the weight $1/(1 + \alpha\tilde s_i)$, and all weights are then divided by their mean. A proportional move takes the label part of the way to a target $t_i$,
\begin{equation}
y_i' = y_i + \alpha\,\tilde s_i\,(t_i - y_i),
\label{eq:noise:move}
\end{equation}
so the most suspect label moves a share $\alpha$ of the way and the least suspect does not move. The target is the weighted mean of the molecule's neighbors' labels \citep{nadaraya1964estimating, watson1964smooth}, or an out-of-fold LightGBM prediction, which is close to the target blending of \citet{reed2014training}. One further rule moves a share $0.5\tilde s_i/(1 + 0.5\tilde s_i)$ of the way to the out-of-fold prediction. The code cites James--Stein shrinkage \citep{james1961estimation} for it, but the variance it estimates cancels, so it is a fixed function of the score.

