Close-pair weights look only at pairs of near-identical molecules. A pair is close if its distance is in the lowest 1\% of all training pairs. The label noise is estimated from the closest fifth of those pairs as $\hat\sigma^2 = \overline{\Delta y^2}/2$, since two noisy copies of one value differ by twice the noise variance. Both molecules of any close pair whose labels differ by more than $3\sqrt{2}\,\hat\sigma$ get weight 0.25. Residual flags give weight 0.25 to the top 5\%, 10\% or 20\% of molecules by out-of-fold LightGBM residual. The same experiment also asks how precise a detector has to be before acting on its flags helps. It builds flag sets of the same size with a known share of truly damaged molecules, from none to all. It then finds the share at which the gain from acting crosses zero. We call that share the break-even precision.

