\paragraph{Robust losses.}
The network is also trained with a Huber loss \citep{Huber1964}, whose threshold is $c$ times 1.4826 times the median absolute out-of-fold residual, with $c$ of 0.5, 1, 1.5 or 2. Under the shift in 10\% sized by model error, each model's own robust loss is tried at $c = 1.5$. That is a Huber loss for LightGBM and the network, a pseudo-Huber loss for XGBoost, and absolute-error splits for the random forest.

% TODO: manual review
% SOURCE: n32_iterative_and_dual_selection.py:240-254, 1171-1195, 1244-1316
\paragraph{Two learners.}
Two learners, LightGBM and an SVM, each score every molecule by its out-of-fold error. For two rounds, each is refitted on the molecules the other finds easiest. Each then keeps its own easiest 80\%, 90\% or 95\%, and the downstream model is fitted on the molecules both keep. This follows the exchange of co-teaching \citep{Han2018}, done between whole refits rather than inside one training loop. Its control deletes the same number of molecules at random, at three seeds.
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] FOR ADELAIDE: the signed-off list puts two learners under noise-robust training. By the outline's test it is a detector followed by deletion (mitigation). Placed here as signed off.

% TODO: manual review
% SOURCE: n29_document_bias.py:28-31, 116-118, 611-612; n19:451-493, 702-716, 1422-1447
\paragraph{Repeat measurements.}
On hERG IC$_{50}$, four methods change what the model sees rather than the labels. The first trains on one row per measurement instead of one row per molecule with the mean label. The second adds the log of each molecule's measurement count as an extra feature. The third gives NGBoost the same extra feature. The fourth fits an XGBoost accelerated-failure-time model \citep{Barnwal2022} to the interval between each molecule's lowest and highest measurement, instead of to one value.


\subsection{How each group is tested, and QM9 as the clean control}\label{sec:noise:tests}

% TODO: manual review
Each group has its own test, because each makes a different claim.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Noise detection.} Is the suspicion score high on the damaged molecules? This can only be read where some labels were damaged and others were not (Section~\ref{sec:noise:detection}). On QM9 with no noise added, every flag is a false flag.
    \item \textbf{Noise mitigation, selected by a detector or a flag.} The held-out score after the action, against the same model on the same features with no action. Where a control ran, the action must also beat the same action on molecules chosen at random.
    \item \textbf{Noise mitigation on every molecule.} Against no action. No random selection is possible, so QM9 with no noise added is the only check.
    \item \textbf{Noise-robust training.} Against the same model trained normally on the same labels. A gain on QM9 with no noise added comes from the training change alone, and we set it beside the gain under damage.
\end{itemize}

