QM9 is the clean-label control. Its HOMO--LUMO gaps are computed rather than measured \citep{Ramakrishnan2014}, so they carry no assay error, and it is the dataset Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} rests on. A method that improves QM9 with no noise added is not removing noise. It is changing the model in a way that would help on any labels. So every results table carries each method's QM9 score with no noise added, before and after the method. Deleting molecules is the cleanest case. If deleting never helps on QM9's clean labels, a gain from deleting on a damaged dataset is noise removal. Methods that weight or move every label may help QM9 too, and their gains elsewhere are read against that. The QM9 result for each method is given with the method in the results. Changing the model, cleaning before the search and the Huber teacher blend have no QM9 result yet.

% TODO: manual review
% SOURCE: n30_censored_likelihood.py:243, 459-470; old thesis text, eq:recovery, checked against n11:300, 1457, 1511 on 2026-09-29
Where a clean score exists, we also report how much of the lost score a method wins back. Each model $m$ is fitted to the clean, the damaged and the treated training labels, giving held-out scores $s^{m}_{\text{clean}}$, $s^{m}_{\text{damaged}}$ and $s^{m}_{\text{treated}}$ in the dataset's own metric. The recovery is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Recovery}_m = 100 \times \frac{s^{m}_{\text{treated}} - s^{m}_{\text{damaged}}}{s^{m}_{\text{clean}} - s^{m}_{\text{damaged}}}\ \%,
\label{eq:recovery}
\end{equation}
which reads the same whether higher or lower values of the metric are better. It is left undefined when the gap between the clean and damaged score is 2\% of the clean score or less. We report it for single representations only. A hybrid's features are re-ranked on the damaged labels, so its clean score is not the score of the same features, and the denominator would mix two changes.

