Knowing which labels are clipped is what helps. Knowing how far each one should go is not. The per-molecule lift raises each clipped label by its own estimated amount, and its control deals the same amounts to the wrong clipped molecules. On LogD, the network on the hybrid scores 0.4988 with each molecule's own lift, and 0.4714, 0.4673 and 0.4632 with the lifts shuffled. On hERG $K_i$ the numbers are 0.7016 against 0.6776, 0.6945 and 0.6704, and on Caco-2 0.2878 against 0.2750, 0.2734 and 0.2708. The shuffled lifts win on all three. On QM9 the molecule's own lift, 0.4481, beats one shuffle of three (0.4495) and loses to the other two. So the gain comes from raising the flagged labels by about the right average amount, and the constant lift does exactly that.

