Real assay labels are not noisy in the way most studies assume. Measurements pooled across laboratories differ mainly between laboratories \citep{Bentz2013}, and labels near an assay's detection limit are clipped to it \citep{Svensson2025} (Table~\ref{tab:noise_sources}). In Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}, these two systematic kinds of error, simulated as grouped-shifted noise and censoring, gave the lowest \aucnorm{}. Gaussian noise, the usual assumption, harmed the models less, but we argue it is the hardest case to correct, because every label is off by an unknown amount in an unknown direction; in the exploratory study on QM9, cleaning usually left a model worse than no cleaning (Sections~\ref{sec:lnl_motivation} and~\ref{sec:flaws:neighbors}).

