Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} found, on all four datasets, that the representation explained a larger share of the variance in predictive performance than in robustness (\aucnorm), where model family dominated and the representation explained at most 11.2\% (``Conclusions'', Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}). Changing the shape of the noise distribution barely changed performance, while grouped-shifted noise and censoring, the two conditions with systematic error, gave the lowest \aucnorm{}. RF and QRF were reliably robust on every dataset, although their predictive performance on clean labels was middling, and on the three assay datasets a Bayesian network gained robustness over its deterministic counterpart at a cost in clean predictive performance. Mean predicted uncertainty rose with noise for almost every probabilistic model on QM9 and for most of them on the assay datasets. The Bayesian networks with a variance head and the heteroscedastic VBLL networks mostly attributed the added noise to the aleatoric component, whereas QRF attributed much of it to the epistemic component. Outside censoring, no model's per-molecule uncertainty identified the corrupted labels, and under censoring the models were most confident on the clipped labels.

