The limits of the hybrid search itself are in the Limitations section of Chapter~\ref{chap:method}. This thesis set out to mitigate label noise, and we conclude that correcting it without ground truth is a hard problem with no clear general solution. Every experimental label carries an unknown amount of noise, so there is no clean copy of the labels to check a correction against, and finding a noisy label may be easier than correcting it \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: Chapter 6 detection results]}. Systematic errors of known structure may still be detectable: under censoring, dividing a molecule's error by its predicted uncertainty picked out the clipped labels better than the error alone (Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}). Whether outliers and shifts shared by one source can be detected is \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: Chapter 6 detection results]}. In the assay experiments, ``clean'' means no noise was added, not that the labels are true; only QM9 is close to noise-free. Real data mix the injected noise conditions (Table~\ref{tab:noise_conditions}) in unknown amounts, so a method that works under one condition may fail on real data. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: per noise condition, methods beating the uncorrected model; negative recovery.]}

